{"chunk_id": "9e0bd2b97708", "text": "In the final book of the Mahabharata, Yudhishthira reaches Heaven but is shocked to see Duryodhana honored there. Refusing to share bliss with the man he blames for the war, he demands to see his brothers and the other fallen heroes. Led away by a celestial guide, he instead encounters a horrifying hellish path where suffering souls call out to him\u2014revealing themselves as Karna, the Pandavas, and Draupadi. Refusing to abandon them, Yudhishthira chooses to remain in hell for their sake. The gods then reveal the scene as a final test of his righteousness: the torment was an illusion meant to complete the small remainder of his karmic debt. After bathing in the heavenly Ganga, he gains a celestial form and is reunited with the heroes of the epic in their divine states. The text closes by explaining the ultimate destinies of major warriors and praising the Mahabharata as a sacred, purifying history.\nIn heaven Yudhishthira sees Duryodhana honored, insists on finding his kin, endures a vision of hell for them, then learns it was a divine test before reunion.\nThemes: heaven and hell, moral testing, compassion and loyalty, karma and justice, illusion of divine trial", "vector": [0.0060402266681194305, -0.02763145975768566, -0.0008323179790750146, -0.005534935276955366, -0.007207050919532776, 0.03087596222758293, -0.016980450600385666, -0.00329602905549109, -0.03087596222758293, -0.00385617115534842, 0.012213424779474735, 0.010724144987761974, 0.013735947199165821, 0.01934734173119068, -0.0032744212076067924, 0.00203778687864542, 0.04850797355175018, -0.015464575961232185, 0.020544083788990974, -0.028509071096777916, -0.006236359477043152, -0.010910304263234138, -0.00807801354676485, 0.012765255756676197, -0.019054803997278214, 0.018815455958247185, 0.025011923164129257, -0.0321790836751461, 0.016913963481783867, -0.001523353741504252, -0.014108267612755299, 0.0059006065130233765, 0.00245498470030725, -0.01636878214776516, -0.0341